\kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9                                      College Administration System Report}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 STUDENT DETAILS}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Name: Neil Vikram Khare}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 PRN: 1262240702}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Class: TY CSE}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Division: A}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 ABSTRACT}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 The College Administration System is a comprehensive software solution designed to streamline, manage, and automate the daily operations of an educational institution. The primary objective of this project is to provide a unified platform for managing student records, faculty details, course enrollments, examination schedules, and other administrative tasks. This project evolved from a standard console-based application into a robust multi-interface system. It now offers three distinct ways to interact with the system: a console-based execution for quick backend testing, a Desktop GUI application for administrative staff, and a modern Web-based Portal for distributed access.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 1. INTRODUCTION}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Educational institutions require robust Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) systems to handle increasing volumes of data regarding students, faculty, and academics. The College Administration System was developed to address these needs by replacing decentralized paper-based and spreadsheet records with a centralized, automated framework. By utilizing modern software engineering practices, this project provides a scalable architecture that can adapt to the evolving demands of university administration.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 2. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE AND MODULES}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 The project architecture is divided into several cohesive parts, each handling a specific domain of college administration.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 2.1 Core Entities (Data Models)}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Student Module: The Student Module, implemented via the Student.java class, serves as a foundational data entity designed to manage and persist student records within the College Administration System. Architected as a modern Java Persistence API (JPA) object, the class is marked with the @Entity annotation, allowing it to seamlessly map its properties to a relational database table, while utilizing an auto-incrementing @Id to ensure every student possesses a unique database identifier. The module encapsulates essential student attributes\uc1 \u8212 ?specifically name, age, and a concatenated string of enrolled courses\uc1 \u8212 ?which are initialized through a parameterized constructor and securely accessed via standard getter and setter methods. Beyond simple data storage, the class includes specific behavioral methods such as enrollInCourse(Course course), which dynamically appends new course names to the student's academic profile, and a getdata() function that outputs the formatted profile to the console for backend verification. By combining robust object-oriented encapsulation with Spring Data JPA integration, this module acts as the crucial link between the system's runtime memory and persistent backend storage for all student-related operations.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Faculty Module: The Faculty Module, defined within the Faculty.java class, functions as a core data entity responsible for modeling and managing the institution's teaching and administrative staff. Designed to be fully compatible with the Java Persistence API (JPA), the class is declared with the @Entity annotation, enabling it to map directly to a backend relational database, utilizing an auto-generated dbId primary key for structural database integrity. The object encapsulates vital professional and personal attributes\uc1 \u8212 ?including the faculty member\uc1 \u8217 ?s name, age, assigned department, official designation (such as "Professor" or "Lecturer"), and an internal institutional ID. It features both a default constructor required by the ORM framework and a parameterized constructor that allows the system to rapidly instantiate new staff records during runtime. Through a comprehensive suite of getter and setter methods, this module provides a secure and structured mechanism for the broader application\uc1 \u8212 ?particularly the Desktop GUI and Web Portal\uc1 \u8212 ?to seamlessly retrieve, update, and display faculty profiles across the College Administration System.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Course Module: The Course Module, encapsulated within the Course.java class, acts as the structural blueprint for defining and managing the academic offerings of the institution. Engineered as a robust data model, the class leverages the @Entity annotation to ensure its properties can be seamlessly persisted to a backend relational database, relying on an auto-generated primary key (@Id) for accurate data indexing. The module securely houses fundamental academic attributes, specifically the courseName, a unique alphanumeric courseCode , and the associated academic credits. It is equipped with a default constructor to satisfy Java Persistence API (JPA) requirements, alongside a parameterized constructor that allows the broader application to efficiently create and load course catalogs into memory. By exposing these encapsulated fields through standard getter and setter methods, the Course Module allows the system\uc1 \u8217 ?s Desktop GUI and Spring Boot Web Portal to dynamically fetch, manipulate, and display curriculum data to both administrators and students.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 2.2 Operational and Administrative Modules}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Admission Module: The Admission module in the College Administration System handles the onboarding of new students by capturing essential enrollment details and providing real-time administrative feedback. At its core, the backend Admission.java class features a getdata method that accepts the student's name, age, assigned roll number, and starting semester, subsequently logging a successful admission confirmation to the console. This backend logic is seamlessly integrated into the administrative Desktop GUI within CollegeERP.java, where a dedicated panel titled "Run Admission Process" is provided. When an administrator clicks the "Simulate Admission" button, an event listener instantiates the Admission class and passes simulated student data into the method, immediately updating a read-only text area on the dashboard with a success message. In its current state, the module serves as a highly effective, event-driven proof-of-concept demonstrating how backend data processing interacts with frontend Java Swing components to streamline and record administrative tasks.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Examination Module: The Examination module in the College Administration System is designed to manage and record the academic performance of students across various assessments. The backend functionality is driven by the Examination.java class, which utilizes a parameterized constructor to initialize and store critical exam data, including the student's name, roll number, an array of subject names, an array of corresponding marks, and the specific type of exam (such as "Midterm" or "Final"). The class also provides getdata() and showdata() methods to output these academic records to the system console for backend verification. On the frontend, this module is integrated into the CollegeERP.java Desktop GUI under a dedicated "Run Examination Process" panel. When an administrator clicks the "Simulate Examination" button, an event listener dynamically instantiates the Examination class with sample student grades\uc1 \u8212 ?such as scores for Math and Physics\uc1 \u8212 ?and immediately appends a formatted summary of the recorded exam and marks to a read-only text log on the dashboard. This setup effectively demonstrates how the system captures complex arrays of academic data in the backend and provides instant, clear visual confirmations to the administrative staff through the graphical interface.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Attendance Module: The Attendance module in the College Administration System is a streamlined component dedicated to tracking and managing student presence over the course of an academic year. At its core, the Attendance.java class serves as a robust data model that defines essential attributes including the student's unique roll number, full name, the current academic year, and their cumulative attendance percentage stored as a floating-point value. The module utilizes a parameterized constructor to efficiently initialize these variables when a new attendance record is created. To facilitate data interaction, the class implements a getdata() method\uc1 \u8212 ?which acts as a prompt interface for data entry verification\uc1 \u8212 ?and a showdata() method that clearly formats and outputs the student's complete attendance profile to the console. Currently executed via the main administrative thread in College.java, this module effectively demonstrates foundational object-oriented principles by encapsulating student attendance metrics and providing a straightforward mechanism for administrators to accurately record and retrieve yearly attendance percentages.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Timetable and Syllabus Modules: The Timetable and Syllabus modules in the College Administration System work in tandem to manage and schedule the institution's academic curriculum. The Syllabus.java class acts as the foundational repository for course content, utilizing a String array to store a dynamic list of subjects (such as Mathematics and Science) initialized directly through its parameterized constructor. Once the curriculum is defined, the Timetable.java class handles the logistical scheduling by mapping these subjects to physical resources and time slots. Instead of a constructor, the Timetable module utilizes a dedicated getdata method to dynamically assign a course name, unique course ID, assigned faculty member, and the specific day of the week to an empty schedule block. Both classes feature a showdata() method that outputs their respective stored data to the system console, allowing administrators to seamlessly verify that the correct subjects have been bundled into the syllabus and properly scheduled with the appropriate professors throughout the academic week.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Reviews Module: The Reviews module in the College Administration System provides a streamlined feedback mechanism for students and faculty to evaluate academic experiences. Implemented through the Reviews.java class, the module captures essential evaluation data using three core string and integer variables: the reviewer's name, the academic year the review was submitted, and the actual text of the review. The class utilizes method overloading to handle data management efficiently, featuring a parameterized getdata(String n, int y, String r) method that acts as a setter to securely assign incoming feedback to the class variables without requiring a constructor. A second, parameterless getdata() method acts as the display function, formatting and printing the stored reviewer name, year, and feedback text directly to the console. Executed within the main administrative thread, this module demonstrates foundational object-oriented encapsulation and method overloading, providing a simple yet effective backend structure for administrators to record, process, and display institutional feedback.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 2.3 Database and Concurrency}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Database Thread Module: The Database Thread Module, encapsulated within the CollegeDatabaseThread.java file, serves as a backend simulation designed to model the strict lifecycle and resource management of a relational database connection. The module is divided into two parts: the CollegeDatabase class, which acts as the blueprint for database behaviors, and the CollegeDatabaseThread main class, which orchestrates the execution flow. When triggered, the system simulates active data querying via the runOperations() method before meticulously executing a sequence of teardown procedures\uc1 \u8212 ?calling closeDatabase(), closeConnection(), closeStatement(), and closeResultSet()\uc1 \u8212 ?to mimic the prevention of memory leaks in a real-world JDBC environment. Finally, it runs a series of status-check methods to verify and print to the console that all connections, statements, and result sets have been securely terminated. Although currently acting as a mock simulator using console output, this module establishes the foundational architectural pattern required for safely handling asynchronous SQL database transactions and resource deallocation within the broader College Administration ERP system.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 2.4 User Interfaces}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Desktop GUI Application: The Desktop GUI Application, implemented via the CollegeERP.java class, provides a comprehensive and interactive frontend dashboard for administrative staff utilizing the Java Swing framework. Engineered for a seamless user experience, the application window is structurally divided into a sleek, dark-themed navigation sidebar on the left and a dynamic main content area managed by a CardLayout on the right, allowing users to instantly switch between Dashboard, Students, Faculty, Courses, and Admissions views without opening new windows. The data-driven panels\uc1 \u8212 ?such as Student and Faculty\uc1 \u8212 ?are constructed using BorderLayout and feature scrollable JTable components powered by DefaultTableModels to cleanly display existing records. Beneath these tables, dedicated input panels equipped with JTextField components and event-driven action buttons allow administrators to dynamically add new entities, which immediately re-render on the tables via a robust refreshTables() method. Furthermore, the specialized Admissions & Exams panel utilizes interactive buttons and read-only JTextArea logs to visually simulate backend processing, culminating in a highly functional, locally hosted desktop ERP interface that bridges complex Java backend logic with user-friendly data management.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Web Portal Application: The Web Portal Application represents the modernization of the College Administration System, transforming it from a localized desktop tool into a globally accessible web service using the Spring Boot framework. At its core, the CollegeWebApplication.java file acts as the primary configuration and entry point, bootstrapping the embedded web server and application context via the @SpringBootApplication annotation. The routing and business logic are handled by CollegeController.java, a RESTful controller that manages in-memory data lists for students and courses. Using standard @GetMapping annotations, this controller intercepts HTTP requests and dynamically constructs and returns raw HTML strings injected with CSS styling. The root endpoint (/) serves an interactive, styled dashboard with navigation links, while the /students and /courses endpoints utilize Java StringBuilder logic to iterate through backend object lists and seamlessly render them into formatted web tables. By leveraging Spring Web and Spring Data architectures, this module effectively bridges Java backend POJOs with frontend web browsers, laying the foundation for a scalable, cloud-ready educational ERP platform.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 3. TOOLS, LIBRARIES, AND TECHNOLOGIES USED}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 The project incorporates modern software engineering tools and frameworks to ensure scalability, maintainability, and ease of deployment.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Programming Language: Java serves as the foundational programming language for the entire College Administration System, providing a robust, platform-independent architecture that unifies the project's backend logic, desktop interface, and web portal. Utilizing JDK 17, the system heavily leverages Java's strict Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) paradigms\uc1 \u8212 ?particularly encapsulation and modularity\uc1 \u8212 ?to securely define core entities like students, faculty, and courses through highly structured Plain Old Java Objects (POJOs). Beyond basic data modeling, Java\uc1 \u8217 ?s versatile standard library empowers the system to execute complex, multi-threaded operations, as seen in the simulated database concurrency module, while its Swing and AWT packages directly drive the interactive graphical components of the local administrative desktop dashboard. By acting as the underlying engine for the powerful Spring Boot framework, Java ensures that the administrative software is not only strongly typed, secure, and easily maintainable, but also highly scalable, effortlessly bridging the gap between localized console execution and a globally accessible web service.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Frameworks and Libraries: }{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Java Swing:The Java Swing framework serves as the technological foundation for the College Administration System's robust desktop application, providing a rich, event-driven graphical user interface (GUI) for administrative staff. By leveraging Swing's lightweight windowing toolkit, the system constructs its primary container using a JFrame, which acts as the main dashboard window. Inside this frame, advanced layout managers are heavily utilized; a BorderLayout structures the overarching view, while a CardLayout enables seamless, dynamic switching between distinct administrative modules\uc1 \u8212 ?such as Students, Faculty, and Courses\uc1 \u8212 ?without the need to open multiple overlapping windows. The interface is highly data-centric, employing JScrollPane-wrapped JTable components powered by DefaultTableModels to cleanly present tabular data, alongside interactive JTextField inputs and JButton elements equipped with ActionListeners to capture user interactions in real-time. Through custom fonts, strategic border padding, and split-pane text logs simulating backend processes, the Java Swing implementation successfully bridges the gap between raw backend Java logic and a highly intuitive, visually organized administrative dashboard.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 AWT: The Abstract Window Toolkit (AWT) operates as the critical underlying architecture supporting the Java Swing components within the College Administration System's desktop interface. While Swing provides the modernized, lightweight visual elements like buttons and tables, it inherently relies on AWT to manage the fundamental spatial organization, styling, and event-handling mechanisms of the dashboard. In this project, AWT's robust layout managers\uc1 \u8212 ?specifically BorderLayout, FlowLayout, GridLayout, and CardLayout\uc1 \u8212 ?are utilized extensively to dynamically dictate how interface panels are positioned and resized relative to one another across different screen dimensions. Furthermore, the system taps into AWT\uc1 \u8217 ?s graphical classes, such as Font and Color, to apply custom styling to text and backgrounds, enhancing the overall readability and aesthetic of the ERP portal. Most importantly, AWT provides the underlying event-delegation model; its event listener interfaces intercept mouse clicks and keystrokes on the Swing components, allowing the frontend application to immediately trigger backend administrative processes like adding a student or simulating an examination.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Spring Boot: Spring Boot functions as the transformative engine of the College Administration System, elevating it from a localized desktop tool into a highly scalable, modern web application. By leveraging the @SpringBootApplication annotation, the framework provides automatic configuration, instantly bootstrapping an embedded Apache Tomcat web server without the need for complex, manual XML deployments. The system utilizes the spring-boot-starter-web module to instantiate the CollegeController, a @RestController that maps HTTP GET requests to specific Java methods, seamlessly serving dynamic HTML views for the home dashboard, student rosters, and course catalogs directly to a web browser. Furthermore, it integrates spring-boot-starter-data-jpa to abstract away tedious SQL queries, effortlessly mapping the project's data models to an embedded relational database. By consolidating dependency management, application context setup, and server execution into a streamlined architecture, Spring Boot empowers the administrative software to be lightweight, easily deployable, and fundamentally prepared for future integration into cloud-based, enterprise-level environments.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Database: The database architecture of the College Administration System leverages modern Spring Boot integrations to provide a lightweight, efficient, and highly scalable data persistence layer. During local development and testing, the system utilizes the H2 Database\uc1 \u8212 ?an embedded, in-memory relational database configured directly via the project's pom.xml file. This eliminates the need for complex external server setups while allowing the application to instantly spin up database tables upon execution and clear them upon shutdown. To bridge the gap between Java objects and SQL tables, the project employs Spring Data JPA (Java Persistence API) as its Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) framework. By simply tagging core POJOs\uc1 \u8212 ?such as Student, Faculty, and Course\uc1 \u8212 ?with @Entity annotations, the system automatically translates class attributes into structured database columns and manages auto-incrementing primary keys. This abstraction not only streamlines backend data manipulation but also ensures that the application is inherently scalable, meaning the lightweight H2 database can be effortlessly swapped out for a robust, enterprise-grade database like MySQL or PostgreSQL in a production environment with minimal to no code refactoring.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Build and Dependency Management: The build lifecycle and dependency management for the College Administration System are entirely orchestrated by Apache Maven, governed by the central pom.xml configuration file. Serving as the structural backbone of the project, Maven automates the resolution, downloading, and versioning of all external libraries required for the system to run, completely eliminating the need to manually install JAR files. The configuration leverages the spring-boot-starter-parent to ensure version compatibility across critical dependencies, pulling in specialized modules such as spring-boot-starter-web for RESTful routing, spring-boot-starter-data-jpa for database mapping, and the h2 database engine for local data storage. Furthermore, the build architecture integrates powerful execution tools\uc1 \u8212 ?specifically the spring-boot-maven-plugin and the exec-maven-plugin\uc1 \u8212 ?which allow the system to seamlessly repackage the code into an executable standalone format and directly boot the web application from the terminal. This standardized, centralized approach to dependency management ensures that the project remains highly reproducible, easily maintainable, and seamlessly compilable across different developer environments.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 4. ADVANTAGES OF THE SYSTEM}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Centralized Data Management: Eliminates paper-based records and isolated Excel sheets by centralizing all college data.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Multiple Access Points: Offers both a Desktop GUI for intensive on-site administrative work and a Web Portal for remote access.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 High Scalability: By utilizing Spring Boot and JPA, the application can easily be connected to a robust production database with minimal code changes.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 User-Friendly Interface: The Swing GUI uses a modern layout with a sleek navigation sidebar, making it highly intuitive for non-technical staff.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 5. LIMITATIONS}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 In-Memory Database Storage: Currently, the system uses an H2 in-memory database, meaning data is lost when the application restarts. A persistent database is required for production deployment.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Dynamic Frontend Rendering: The web application currently returns hardcoded HTML strings from the Spring controller rather than using a dedicated templating engine.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Lack of Authentication: The system does not currently implement login credentials, role-based access control, or security measures.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 6. REAL-WORLD APPLICATIONS}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Universities and Colleges: Can be used as a primary ERP software to manage day-to-day administrative tasks.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 Schools and Coaching Institutes: The modules are flexible enough to be adapted for smaller educational centers to manage student admissions and timetable scheduling.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 7. CONCLUSION}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 The College Administration System is a well-rounded Java project that demonstrates a clear progression from core object-oriented programming concepts to advanced framework implementations. By integrating Java Swing for desktop operations and Spring Boot for web accessibility, the project serves as a comprehensive proof-of-concept for a modern educational ERP system. With future enhancements in security and database persistence, it can be deployed as a fully functional, enterprise-grade application.}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang9 \langnp9 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \pnrnot0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \ls1 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\listtext \pard \plain \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \f1 8. }{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 Github link- }{\field \fldedit {\*\fldinst {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 }{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang2057 \langnp2057  HYPERLINK "https://github.com/neilvikramkhare-beep/College-System" }}{\fldrslt {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 }{\cs15 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \cf13 \ul https://github.com/neilvikramkhare-beep/College-System}}}{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 }{\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \par }\pard \plain \ql \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa200 \lisa0 \pnrnot0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \ls0 \fs21 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 {\fs22 \kerning2 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af4 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \par }}